      "question": "What is Indian national space and aeronautics agency?",
      "answer": "ISRO",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": "The Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO ) is the national space agency of India, headquartered in Bengaluru, Karnataka.  It serves as the principal research and development arm of the Department of Space (DoS), overseen by the Prime Minister of India, with the Chairman of ISRO also working as the chief executive of the DoS.  It is primarily responsible for space-based operations, space exploration, international space cooperation and the development of related technologies.  The agency maintains a constellation of imaging, communications and remote sensing satellites"
    },
    {
      "id": "q111",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "ISRO & Space",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-27T15:52:24.345Z",
      "subject": "ISRO & Space",
      "subSubject": "",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "ISRO built India's first satellite, Aryabhata, which was launched by the Soviet space agency Interkosmos in _____",
      "answer": "1975",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " It was renamed as ISRO in 1969 and was subsumed into the Department of Atomic Energy (DAE).  In 1972, the Government set up a space commission and the DoS bringing ISRO under its purview.  It has since then been managed by the DoS, which also governs various other institutions in the domain of astronomy and space technology.  ISRO built India's first satellite, Aryabhata, which was launched by the Soviet space agency Interkosmos in 1975.  In 1980, it launched the satellite RS-1 on board the indigenously built launch vehicle SLV-3, making India the seventh country to undertake orbital launches.  It has subsequently developed various small-lift and medium-lift launch vehicles, enabling the agency to launch various satellites and deep space missions.  ISRO has full launch capabilities with the ability to deploy cryogenic engines, launch extraterrestrial missions and artificial satellites"
    },
    {
      "id": "q112",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "ISRO & Space",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-27T15:52:24.345Z",
      "subject": "ISRO & Space",
      "subSubject": "",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "In _____, it launched the satellite RS-1 on board the indigenously built launch vehicle SLV-3, making India the seventh country to undertake orbital launches",
      "answer": "1980",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In 1972, the Government set up a space commission and the DoS bringing ISRO under its purview.  It has since then been managed by the DoS, which also governs various other institutions in the domain of astronomy and space technology.  ISRO built India's first satellite, Aryabhata, which was launched by the Soviet space agency Interkosmos in 1975.  In 1980, it launched the satellite RS-1 on board the indigenously built launch vehicle SLV-3, making India the seventh country to undertake orbital launches.  It has subsequently developed various small-lift and medium-lift launch vehicles, enabling the agency to launch various satellites and deep space missions.  ISRO has full launch capabilities with the ability to deploy cryogenic engines, launch extraterrestrial missions and artificial satellites.  It is also the only one of the few governmental space agencies to have demonstrated unmanned landing capabilities"
    },
    {
      "id": "q113",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "ISRO & Space",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-27T15:52:24.345Z",
      "subject": "ISRO & Space",
      "subSubject": "",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Bhabha, who established the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research in _____",
      "answer": "1945",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Later, Indian scientists like C.  Raman and Meghnad Saha contributed to scientific principles applicable in space sciences.  After 1945, important developments were made in coordinated space research in India by two scientists: Vikram Sarabhai, founder of the Physical Research Laboratory at Ahmedabad, and Homi J.  Bhabha, who established the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research in 1945.  Initial experiments in space sciences included the study of cosmic radiation, high-altitude and airborne testing, deep underground experimentation at the Kolar mines—one of the deepest mining sites in the world—and studies of the upper atmosphere.  These studies were done at research laboratories, universities, and independent locations.  In 1950, the DAE was founded with Bhabha as its secretary"
    },
    {
      "id": "q114",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "ISRO & Space",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-27T15:52:24.345Z",
      "subject": "ISRO & Space",
      "subSubject": "",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "In _____, the DAE was founded with Bhabha as its secretary",
      "answer": "1950",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Bhabha, who established the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research in 1945.  Initial experiments in space sciences included the study of cosmic radiation, high-altitude and airborne testing, deep underground experimentation at the Kolar mines—one of the deepest mining sites in the world—and studies of the upper atmosphere.  These studies were done at research laboratories, universities, and independent locations.  In 1950, the DAE was founded with Bhabha as its secretary.  It provided funding for space research throughout India.  During this time, tests continued on aspects of meteorology and the Earth's magnetic field, a topic that had been studied in India since the establishment of the Colaba Observatory in 1823.  In 1954, the Aryabhatta Research Institute of Observational Sciences (ARIES) was established in the foothills of the Himalayas"
    },
    {
      "id": "q115",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "ISRO & Space",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-27T15:52:24.345Z",
      "subject": "ISRO & Space",
      "subSubject": "",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "During this time, tests continued on aspects of meteorology and the Earth's magnetic field, a topic that had been studied in India since the establishment of the Colaba Observatory",
      "answer": "1823",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " These studies were done at research laboratories, universities, and independent locations.  In 1950, the DAE was founded with Bhabha as its secretary.  It provided funding for space research throughout India.  During this time, tests continued on aspects of meteorology and the Earth's magnetic field, a topic that had been studied in India since the establishment of the Colaba Observatory in 1823.  In 1954, the Aryabhatta Research Institute of Observational Sciences (ARIES) was established in the foothills of the Himalayas.  The Rangpur Observatory was set up in 1957 at Osmania University, Hyderabad.  Space research was further encouraged by the government of India"
    },
    {
      "id": "q116",
