      "answer": "Epic Sanskrit",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In 1913, Bengali poet Rabindranath Tagore became India's first Nobel laureate in literature.  == In archaic and ancient Indian languages == === Sanskrit literature === ==== Vedic literature ==== Examples of early works written in Vedic Sanskrit include, the core Vedas and Upanishads.  Other examples include the Sulba Sutras, which are some of the earliest texts on geometry.  ==== Epic Sanskrit literature ==== Ved Vyasa's Mahabharata and Valmiki's Ramayana, written in Epic Sanskrit, are regarded as the greatest Sanskrit epics.  ==== Classical Sanskrit literature ==== The famous poet and playwright Kālidāsa wrote one epic: Raghuvamsha (Dynasty of Raghu); it was written in Classical Sanskrit rather than Epic Sanskrit.  Other examples of works written in Classical Sanskrit include the Pāṇini's Ashtadhyayi, which standardised the grammar and phonetics of Classical Sanskrit.  The Laws of Manu (मनुस्मृति) is a famous text in Hinduism"
    },
    {
      "id": "q168",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Books & Authors",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-27T15:53:36.563Z",
      "subject": "Books & Authors",
      "subSubject": "",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "==== _____ literature ==== The famous poet and playwright Kālidāsa wrote one epic: Raghuvamsha (Dynasty of Raghu); it was written in Classical Sanskrit rather than Epic Sanskrit",
      "answer": "Classical Sanskrit",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " == In archaic and ancient Indian languages == === Sanskrit literature === ==== Vedic literature ==== Examples of early works written in Vedic Sanskrit include, the core Vedas and Upanishads.  Other examples include the Sulba Sutras, which are some of the earliest texts on geometry.  ==== Epic Sanskrit literature ==== Ved Vyasa's Mahabharata and Valmiki's Ramayana, written in Epic Sanskrit, are regarded as the greatest Sanskrit epics.  ==== Classical Sanskrit literature ==== The famous poet and playwright Kālidāsa wrote one epic: Raghuvamsha (Dynasty of Raghu); it was written in Classical Sanskrit rather than Epic Sanskrit.  Other examples of works written in Classical Sanskrit include the Pāṇini's Ashtadhyayi, which standardised the grammar and phonetics of Classical Sanskrit.  The Laws of Manu (मनुस्मृति) is a famous text in Hinduism.  Kālidāsa is often considered to be the greatest playwright in Sanskrit literature and one of the greatest poets in Sanskrit literature; his Recognition of Shakuntala (अभिज्ञानशाकुन्तलम्) and Meghaduuta are Kalidasa's most famous play and poem respectively"
    },
    {
      "id": "q169",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Books & Authors",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-27T15:53:36.563Z",
      "subject": "Books & Authors",
      "subSubject": "",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Other examples of works written in _____ include the Pāṇini's Ashtadhyayi, which standardised the grammar and phonetics of Classical Sanskrit",
      "answer": "Classical Sanskrit",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Other examples include the Sulba Sutras, which are some of the earliest texts on geometry.  ==== Epic Sanskrit literature ==== Ved Vyasa's Mahabharata and Valmiki's Ramayana, written in Epic Sanskrit, are regarded as the greatest Sanskrit epics.  ==== Classical Sanskrit literature ==== The famous poet and playwright Kālidāsa wrote one epic: Raghuvamsha (Dynasty of Raghu); it was written in Classical Sanskrit rather than Epic Sanskrit.  Other examples of works written in Classical Sanskrit include the Pāṇini's Ashtadhyayi, which standardised the grammar and phonetics of Classical Sanskrit.  The Laws of Manu (मनुस्मृति) is a famous text in Hinduism.  Kālidāsa is often considered to be the greatest playwright in Sanskrit literature and one of the greatest poets in Sanskrit literature; his Recognition of Shakuntala (अभिज्ञानशाकुन्तलम्) and Meghaduuta are Kalidasa's most famous play and poem respectively.  Other famous plays include Mricchakatika by Shudraka, Svapna Vasavadattam by Bhasa, and Ratnavali by Sri Harsha"
    },
    {
      "id": "q170",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Govt Schemes",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-27T15:54:27.657Z",
      "subject": "Govt Schemes",
