      "question": "Early on, the Europe–Asia boundary was taken to run from the _____ along the Rioni River (known then as the Phasis) in Georgia",
      "answer": "Black Sea",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Division into three parts eventually came to predominate.  From the Greek viewpoint, the Aegean Sea was the center of the world; Asia lay to the east, Europe to the north and west, and Africa to the south.  The boundaries between the continents were not fixed.  Early on, the Europe–Asia boundary was taken to run from the Black Sea along the Rioni River (known then as the Phasis) in Georgia.  Later it was viewed as running from the Black Sea through Kerch Strait, the Sea of Azov and along the Don River (known then as the Tanais) in Russia.  The boundary between Asia and Africa was generally taken to be the Nile River.  Herodotus in the 5th century BCE objected to the whole of Egypt being split between Asia and Africa (\"Libya\") and took the boundary to lie along the western border of Egypt, regarding Egypt as part of Asia"
    },
    {
      "id": "q328",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "World Geography",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-27T16:09:01.540Z",
      "subject": "World Geography",
      "subSubject": "World Geography",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Later it was viewed as running from the _____ through Kerch Strait, the Sea of Azov and along the Don River (known then as the Tanais) in Russia",
      "answer": "Black Sea",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " From the Greek viewpoint, the Aegean Sea was the center of the world; Asia lay to the east, Europe to the north and west, and Africa to the south.  The boundaries between the continents were not fixed.  Early on, the Europe–Asia boundary was taken to run from the Black Sea along the Rioni River (known then as the Phasis) in Georgia.  Later it was viewed as running from the Black Sea through Kerch Strait, the Sea of Azov and along the Don River (known then as the Tanais) in Russia.  The boundary between Asia and Africa was generally taken to be the Nile River.  Herodotus in the 5th century BCE objected to the whole of Egypt being split between Asia and Africa (\"Libya\") and took the boundary to lie along the western border of Egypt, regarding Egypt as part of Asia.  He also questioned the division into three of what is really a single landmass, a debate that continues nearly two and a half millennia later"
    },
    {
      "id": "q329",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "World Geography",
