      "fact": " Latin encyclopaedists of the period such as Isidore of Seville preserved the majority of general ancient knowledge.  In contrast, because the Byzantine Empire resisted attacks from invaders, they were able to preserve and improve prior learning.  John Philoponus, a Byzantine scholar in the 6th century, started to question Aristotle's teaching of physics, introducing the theory of impetus.  His criticism served as an inspiration to medieval scholars and Galileo Galilei, who extensively cited his works ten centuries later.  During late antiquity and the Early Middle Ages, natural phenomena were mainly examined via the Aristotelian approach.  The approach includes Aristotle's four causes: material, formal, moving, and final cause.  Many Greek classical texts were preserved by the Byzantine Empire and Arabic translations were made by Christians, mainly Nestorians and Miaphysites"
