      "question": "The new submarines feature air-independent propulsion and started joining the navy towards the end of _____; four were in service by the end of 2021",
      "answer": "2017",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " === Submarines === The Indian Navy operates a sizeable fleet of Sindhughosh (Russian Kilo-class design) and Shishumar (German Type 209/1500 design)-class submarines.  A nuclear-powered attack submarine INS Chakra has been leased from Russia.  India is completing the construction of six Scorpène-class submarines at Mazagon Dockyards Limited (MDL), in Mumbai, under technology transfer from French firm DCNS.  The new submarines feature air-independent propulsion and started joining the navy towards the end of 2017; four were in service by the end of 2021.  Designed for coastal defence against underwater threats, the 1,750-tonne submarine-submarine-killer (SSK) Scorpène is 67 metres (219 ft 10 in) in length and can dive to a depth of 300 metres (980 ft).  According to French naval officials, the submarine can stay at sea for 45 days with a crew of 31.  The standard version has six torpedo tubes and anti-ship missile launchers"
    },
