      "answer": "Constitutional Advisor",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Ambedkar and I have no doubt that we are grateful to him for having achieved this task in a manner which is undoubtedly commendable.  Ambedkar in his concluding speech in constituent assembly on 25 November 1949 stated that: The credit that is given to me does not really belong to me.  It belongs partly to Sir B.  Rau the Constitutional Advisor to the Constituent Assembly who prepared a rough draft of the Constitution for the consideration of Drafting Committee.  A part of the credit must go to the members of the Drafting Committee who, as I have said, have sat for 141 days and without whose ingenuity to devise new formulae and capacity to tolerate and to accommodate different points of view, the task of framing the Constitution could not have come to so successful a conclusion.  Much greater share of the credit must go to Mr.  Mukherjee, the Chief Draftsman of the Constitution"
    },
