      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ faunas were very similar to those found on Madagascar due to their shared connection until around 90 million years ago",
      "answer": "Late Cretaceous Insular Indian",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": "5% of birds are endemic, contrasting with 45. 8% of reptiles and 55.  The Indian subcontinent was formerly an island landmass (Insular India) that split away from Gondwana around 125 million years ago, during the Early Cretaceous.  Late Cretaceous Insular Indian faunas were very similar to those found on Madagascar due to their shared connection until around 90 million years ago.  The Cretaceous-Paleogene extinction event around 66 million years ago caused the extinction of many animals native to Insular India, such as its titanosaurian and abelisaurid dinosaurs.  During the early Cenozoic era, around 55-50 million years ago, the Indian subcontinent collided with Laurasia, allowing animals from Asia to migrate into the Indian subcontinent.  Some elements of India's modern fauna, such as the frog family Nasikabatrachidae and the caecillian family Chikilidae, are suggested to have been present in India prior to its collision with Asia"
    },
    {
      "id": "q604",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Environment & Ecology",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T12:36:20.799Z",
      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Ostriches were also formerly native to India, but also became extinct during the _____",
      "answer": "Late Pleistocene",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Some elements of India's modern fauna, such as the frog family Nasikabatrachidae and the caecillian family Chikilidae, are suggested to have been present in India prior to its collision with Asia.  Four species of megafauna (large animals) native to India became extinct during the Late Pleistocene around 10,000-50,000 years ago as part of a global wave of megafauna extinctions, these include the very large elephant Palaeoloxodon namadicus (possibly the largest land mammal to have ever lived), the elephant relative Stegodon, the hippopotamus Hexaprotodon, and the equine Equus namadicus.  These extinctions are thought to have been after the arrival of modern humans on the Indian subcontinent.  Ostriches were also formerly native to India, but also became extinct during the Late Pleistocene.  India is home to several well-known large animals, including the Indian elephant, Indian rhinoceros, and Gaur.  India is the only country where the big cats tiger and lion exist in the wild.  Members of the cat family include Bengal tiger, Asiatic lion, Indian leopard, snow leopard, and clouded leopard"
    },
    {
      "id": "q605",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Environment & Ecology",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T12:36:20.799Z",
      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ of Modern India: Land, Population, Technology and Development (Bloomsbury Publishing India, 2022) online review Prasad, S",
      "answer": "Environmental History",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " As of 2023, there are 1022 protected areas including 106 national parks, 573 wildlife sanctuaries, 220 conservation reserves and 123 community reserves.  In addition, there are 55 tiger reserves, 18 biosphere reserves and 32 elephant reserves.  == National symbols == == See also == List of birds of India List of mammals of India List of reptiles of South Asia Wildlife population of India == Notes == == References == == Further reading == Saravanan, Velayutham.  Environmental History of Modern India: Land, Population, Technology and Development (Bloomsbury Publishing India, 2022) online review Prasad, S.  \"Conservation of wetlands of India – a review\".  \"Fungal biodiversity: Distribution, conservation and prospecting of fungi from India\" (PDF).  Archived from the original (PDF) on 28 August 2017"
    },
    {
