      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Society",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T12:38:14.622Z",
      "subject": "Society",
      "subSubject": "Society",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Malik writes _____ saw \"perpetuation of caste status by birth\" and \"caste-class patterns\" were found in Indian society since this period",
      "answer": "Indus Valley Civilization",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Olivelle adds that the overwhelming focus in matters relating to purity/impurity in the Dharma-sastra texts concerns \"individuals irrespective of their varna affiliation\" and all four varnas could attain purity or impurity by the content of their character, ethical intent, actions, innocence or ignorance (acts by children), stipulations, and ritualistic behaviours.  Dumont, in his later publications, acknowledged that ancient varna hierarchy was not based on purity-impurity ranking principle, and that the Vedic literature is devoid of the untouchability concept.  == History == === Early history === The existence of jati and the precursor of caste has been found in the Indus Valley Civilisation (3300–1700 BCE).  Malik writes Indus Valley Civilization saw \"perpetuation of caste status by birth\" and \"caste-class patterns\" were found in Indian society since this period.  Charles Maisels finds caste stratification to have arisen from occupational groups upon the devolution from urban Indus Valley society.  Romila Thapar finds possibility of a caste as pre-Vedic element, and notes that Jati pre-dated Vedic varna.  Thapar further notes that Jatis were derived from clans of Indus Valley C"
    },
    {
      "id": "q666",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Society",
      "region": "",
