      "answer": "Jain Agamas",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Republican communities (such as the community of Rajakumara) are merged into Magadha kingdom.  Villages had their own assemblies under their local chiefs called Gramakas.  Their administrations were divided into executive, judicial, and military functions.  Early sources, from the Buddhist Pāli Canon, the Jain Agamas and the Hindu Puranas, mention Magadha being ruled by the Pradyota dynasty and Haryanka dynasty (c.  544–413 BCE) for some 200 years, c.  King Bimbisara of the Haryanka dynasty led an active and expansive policy, conquering Anga in what is now eastern Bihar and West Bengal.  King Bimbisara was overthrown and killed by his son, Prince Ajatashatru, who continued the expansionist policy of Magadha"
