      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T12:59:09.666Z",
      "subject": "Indian History",
      "subSubject": "Indian History",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Hyderabad was founded by the _____ dynasty of Golconda in 1591",
      "answer": "Qutb Shahi",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Mysore's alliance with the French was seen as a threat to the British East India Company, and Mysore was attacked from all four sides.  The Nizam of Hyderabad and the Marathas launched an invasion from the north.  The British won a decisive victory at the Siege of Seringapatam (1799).  Hyderabad was founded by the Qutb Shahi dynasty of Golconda in 1591.  Following a brief Mughal rule, Asif Jah, a Mughal official, seized control of Hyderabad and declared himself Nizam-al-Mulk of Hyderabad in 1724.  The Nizams lost considerable territory and paid tribute to the Maratha Empire after being routed in multiple battles, such as the Battle of Palkhed.  However, the Nizams maintained their sovereignty from 1724 until 1948 through paying tributes to the Marathas, and later, being vassals of the British"
    },
    {
      "id": "q973",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Indian History",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T12:59:09.667Z",
      "subject": "Indian History",
      "subSubject": "Indian History",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Following a brief Mughal rule, _____, a Mughal official, seized control of Hyderabad and declared himself Nizam-al-Mulk of Hyderabad in 1724",
      "answer": "Asif Jah",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The Nizam of Hyderabad and the Marathas launched an invasion from the north.  The British won a decisive victory at the Siege of Seringapatam (1799).  Hyderabad was founded by the Qutb Shahi dynasty of Golconda in 1591.  Following a brief Mughal rule, Asif Jah, a Mughal official, seized control of Hyderabad and declared himself Nizam-al-Mulk of Hyderabad in 1724.  The Nizams lost considerable territory and paid tribute to the Maratha Empire after being routed in multiple battles, such as the Battle of Palkhed.  However, the Nizams maintained their sovereignty from 1724 until 1948 through paying tributes to the Marathas, and later, being vassals of the British.  Hyderabad State became a princely state in British India in 1798"
    },
