      "subSubject": "World History",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "=== _____ and Berlin Crisis of 1958–1959 === In 1957, Polish foreign minister Adam Rapacki proposed the Rapacki Plan for a nuclear free zone in central Europe",
      "answer": "Rapacki Plan",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In Khrushchev's formulation, peace would allow capitalism to collapse on its own, as well as giving the Soviets time to boost their military capabilities, which remained for decades until Gorbachev's later \"new thinking\" envisioning peaceful coexistence as an end in itself rather than a form of class struggle.  The events in Hungary produced ideological fractures within the communist parties of the world, particularly in Western Europe, with great decline in membership, as many in both western and socialist countries felt disillusioned by the brutal Soviet response.  The communist parties in the West would never recover.  === Rapacki Plan and Berlin Crisis of 1958–1959 === In 1957, Polish foreign minister Adam Rapacki proposed the Rapacki Plan for a nuclear free zone in central Europe.  Public opinion tended to be favourable in the West, but it was rejected by leaders of West Germany, Britain, France and the United States.  They feared it would leave the powerful conventional armies of the Warsaw Pact dominant over the weaker NATO armies.  During November 1958, Khrushchev made an unsuccessful attempt to turn all of Berlin into an independent, demilitarized \"free city\""
    },
    {
      "id": "q1473",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "World History",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T12:59:21.320Z",
      "subject": "World History",
      "subSubject": "World History",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Public opinion tended to be favourable in the West, but it was rejected by leaders of _____, Britain, France and the United States",
      "answer": "West Germany",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The events in Hungary produced ideological fractures within the communist parties of the world, particularly in Western Europe, with great decline in membership, as many in both western and socialist countries felt disillusioned by the brutal Soviet response.  The communist parties in the West would never recover.  === Rapacki Plan and Berlin Crisis of 1958–1959 === In 1957, Polish foreign minister Adam Rapacki proposed the Rapacki Plan for a nuclear free zone in central Europe.  Public opinion tended to be favourable in the West, but it was rejected by leaders of West Germany, Britain, France and the United States.  They feared it would leave the powerful conventional armies of the Warsaw Pact dominant over the weaker NATO armies.  During November 1958, Khrushchev made an unsuccessful attempt to turn all of Berlin into an independent, demilitarized \"free city\".  He gave the United States, Great Britain and France a six-month ultimatum to withdraw their troops from the sectors of West Berlin, or he would transfer control of Western access rights to the East Germans"
    },
