      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The communist parties in the West would never recover.  === Rapacki Plan and Berlin Crisis of 1958–1959 === In 1957, Polish foreign minister Adam Rapacki proposed the Rapacki Plan for a nuclear free zone in central Europe.  Public opinion tended to be favourable in the West, but it was rejected by leaders of West Germany, Britain, France and the United States.  They feared it would leave the powerful conventional armies of the Warsaw Pact dominant over the weaker NATO armies.  During November 1958, Khrushchev made an unsuccessful attempt to turn all of Berlin into an independent, demilitarized \"free city\".  He gave the United States, Great Britain and France a six-month ultimatum to withdraw their troops from the sectors of West Berlin, or he would transfer control of Western access rights to the East Germans.  Khrushchev earlier explained to Mao Zedong that \"Berlin is the testicles of the West"
    },
