      "question": "The same percentage were broadly centrist or unaffiliated, along with two directors, Étienne-François Letourneur and _____",
      "answer": "Lazare Carnot",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Nevertheless, by 1799 the economy had been stabilised, and important reforms made allowing steady expansion of French industry.  Many of these remained in place for much of the 19th century.  Prior to 1797, three of the five directors were firmly Republican; Barras, Révellière-Lépeaux and Jean-François Rewbell, as were around 40% of the legislature.  The same percentage were broadly centrist or unaffiliated, along with two directors, Étienne-François Letourneur and Lazare Carnot.  Although only 20% were committed Royalists, many centrists supported the restoration of the exiled Louis XVIII in the belief this would bring peace.  The elections of May 1797 resulted in significant gains for the right, with Royalists Jean-Charles Pichegru elected president of the Council of 500, and Barthélemy appointed a director.  With Royalists apparently on the verge of power, Republicans attempted a pre-emptive coup on 4 September"
    },
