      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In February 1788, the Society of the Friends of the Blacks was formed in France with the aim of abolishing slavery in the empire.  In August 1789, colonial slave owners and merchants formed the rival Club de Massiac to represent their interests.  When the Constituent Assembly adopted the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen in August 1789, delegates representing the colonial landowners successfully argued that the principles should not apply in the colonies as they would bring economic ruin and disrupt trade.  Colonial landowners also gained control of the Colonial Committee of the Assembly from where they exerted a powerful influence against abolition.  People of colour also faced social and legal discrimination in mainland France and its colonies, including a bar on their access to professions such as law, medicine and pharmacy.  In 1789–1790, a delegation of free coloureds, led by Vincent Ogé and Julien Raimond, unsuccessfully lobbied the Assembly to end discrimination against this group.  Ogé left for Saint-Domingue where an uprising against white landowners broke out in October 1790"
    },
    {
      "id": "q1717",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "World History",
