      "question": "_____, the puppeteer plays a dholak (hand drum) with one hand and manipulates the puppet with the other",
      "answer": "In Orissa",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " With the help of these three fingers, the glove puppet comes alive.  The tradition of glove puppets in India is popular in Uttar Pradesh, Orissa, West Bengal and Kerala.  In Uttar Pradesh, glove puppet plays usually present social themes, whereas in Orissa such plays are based on stories of Radha and Krishna.  In Orissa, the puppeteer plays a dholak (hand drum) with one hand and manipulates the puppet with the other.  The delivery of the dialogue, the movement of the puppet and the beat of the dholak are well synchronised and create a dramatic atmosphere.  In Kerala, the traditional glove puppet play is called Pavakoothu.  === Shadow play === Shadow puppets are an ancient part of India's culture and art, particularly regionally as the keelu bomme and Tholu bommalata of Andhra Pradesh, the Togalu gombeyaata in Karnataka, the charma bahuli natya in Maharashtra, the Ravana chhaya in Odisha, the Tholpavakoothu in Kerala and the thol bommalatta in Tamil Nadu"
    },
    {
      "id": "q1776",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Art & Culture",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T12:59:32.448Z",
      "subject": "Art & Culture",
      "subSubject": "Art & Culture",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____, the traditional glove puppet play is called Pavakoothu",
      "answer": "In Kerala",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In Uttar Pradesh, glove puppet plays usually present social themes, whereas in Orissa such plays are based on stories of Radha and Krishna.  In Orissa, the puppeteer plays a dholak (hand drum) with one hand and manipulates the puppet with the other.  The delivery of the dialogue, the movement of the puppet and the beat of the dholak are well synchronised and create a dramatic atmosphere.  In Kerala, the traditional glove puppet play is called Pavakoothu.  === Shadow play === Shadow puppets are an ancient part of India's culture and art, particularly regionally as the keelu bomme and Tholu bommalata of Andhra Pradesh, the Togalu gombeyaata in Karnataka, the charma bahuli natya in Maharashtra, the Ravana chhaya in Odisha, the Tholpavakoothu in Kerala and the thol bommalatta in Tamil Nadu.  Shadow puppet play is also found in pictorial traditions in India, such as temple mural painting, loose-leaf folio paintings, and the narrative paintings.  Dance forms such as the Chhau of Odisha literally mean \"shadow\""
    },
