      "hint": "",
      "fact": " India had its first general election in 1951, which was won by the Indian National Congress, a political party that went on to dominate subsequent elections until 1977, when a non-Congress government was formed for the first time in independent India.  The 1990s saw the end of single-party domination and the rise of coalition governments.  The latest 18th Lok Sabha elections was conducted in seven phases from 19 April 2024 to 1 June 2024 by the Election commission of India.  The results bought in the NDA (National Democratic Alliance) to form in the next government In recent decades, Indian politics has become a dynastic affair.  Possible reasons for this could be the party stability, absence of party organisations, independent civil society associations that mobilise support for the parties and centralised financing of elections.  According to the V-Dem Democracy indices India in 2023 was the 19th most electoral democratic country in Asia.  == Union government == The Government of India is modelled after the Westminster system"
