      "question": "According to the _____'s 2025 Poverty & Equity Brief, India's extreme poverty rate, measured at the international poverty line of $2",
      "answer": "World Bank",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": "2 billion lower-income individuals, 66 million middle-income individuals, 16 million upper-middle-income individuals, and 2 million in the high-income group.  As per The Economist, 78 million of India's population are considered middle class as of 2017, if defined using the cutoff of those making more than $10 per day, a standard used by the India's National Council of Applied Economic Research.  India has achieved significant reductions in both monetary and multidimensional poverty in recent years.  According to the World Bank's 2025 Poverty & Equity Brief, India's extreme poverty rate, measured at the international poverty line of $2. 15 per day (2017 PPP), declined from 16. 3% in 2022–23, lifting approximately 171 million people out of extreme poverty.  In terms of multidimensional poverty, a United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) report found that India was among 25 countries that halved their global Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI) values within 15 years (2005–06 to 2019–21), with an estimated 415 million people exiting multidimensional poverty during that period"
