      "question": "Efforts to save the lion started before Independence but it was in 1965 that the _____ stepped in to set up a conservation programme",
      "answer": "Indian Forest Service",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " India is home to 75 per cent of the world's wild tiger population.  The big cats like lion, tiger, leopard, cheetah, snow leopard, jaguar and puma were on the brink of extinction.  With hunting and encroachment, their numbers dropped to a mere 20.  Efforts to save the lion started before Independence but it was in 1965 that the Indian Forest Service stepped in to set up a conservation programme.  Researchers believe a combination of factors is driving the unprecedented migration in tigers.  Rising temperatures due to climate change are making the lower foothills less hospitable.  == See also == == References == == Further reading == == External links == Ministry of Finance Ministry of Commerce and Industry Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation India profile Archived 18 March 2021 at the Wayback Machine at the CIA World Factbook India profile at The World Bank India – OECD"
