      "question": "Farmers in developed economies of Europe and the _____ receive 64% to 81%",
      "answer": "United States",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Irrigation systems are inadequate, leading to crop failures in some parts of the country because of lack of water.  In other areas regional floods, poor seed quality and inefficient farming practices, lack of cold storage and harvest spoilage cause over 30% of farmer's produce going to waste, lack of organised retail and competing buyers thereby limiting Indian farmer's ability to sell the surplus and commercial crops.  The Indian farmer receives just 10% to 23% of the price the Indian consumer pays for exactly the same produce, the difference going to losses, inefficiencies and middlemen.  Farmers in developed economies of Europe and the United States receive 64% to 81%.  === Productivity === Although India has attained self-sufficiency in food staples, the productivity of its farms is below that of Brazil, the United States, France and other nations.  Indian wheat farms, for example, produce about a third of the wheat per hectare per year compared to farms in France.  Rice productivity in India was less than half that of China"
    },
