      "question": "_____ is situated in eastern India, covering much of Jharkhand and adjacent parts of Odisha, Bihar and Chhattisgarh",
      "answer": "Chhota Nagpur Plateau",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Plateaus (clockwise from top-left) Malwa Plateau is spread across Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh and Gujarat.  The average elevation of the Malwa plateau is 500 metres, and the landscape generally slopes towards the north.  Most of the region is drained by the Chambal River and its tributaries; the western part is drained by the upper reaches of the Mahi River.  Chhota Nagpur Plateau is situated in eastern India, covering much of Jharkhand and adjacent parts of Odisha, Bihar and Chhattisgarh.  Its total area is approximately 65,000 km2 (25,000 sq mi) and is made up of three smaller plateaus—the Ranchi, Hazaribagh, and Kodarma plateaus.  The Ranchi plateau is the largest, with an average elevation of 700 m (2,300 ft).  Much of the plateau is forested, covered by the Chhota Nagpur dry deciduous forests"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2191",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Geography",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:00:36.937Z",
      "subject": "Geography",
      "subSubject": "Geography",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Much of the plateau is forested, covered by the _____ dry deciduous forests",
      "answer": "Chhota Nagpur",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Chhota Nagpur Plateau is situated in eastern India, covering much of Jharkhand and adjacent parts of Odisha, Bihar and Chhattisgarh.  Its total area is approximately 65,000 km2 (25,000 sq mi) and is made up of three smaller plateaus—the Ranchi, Hazaribagh, and Kodarma plateaus.  The Ranchi plateau is the largest, with an average elevation of 700 m (2,300 ft).  Much of the plateau is forested, covered by the Chhota Nagpur dry deciduous forests.  Vast reserves of metal ores and coal have been found in the Chota Nagpur plateau.  Southern Garanulite terrain: Covers South India especially Tamil Nadu excluding western and eastern ghats.  The Southern Granulite Terrain (SGT) is one of the most geologically significant regions of the Indian Shield"
    },
