      "answer": "Malabar Coast",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Numerous rivers and backwaters inundate the region.  Mostly originating in the Western Ghats, the rivers are fast-flowing, usually perennial, and empty into estuaries.  Major rivers flowing into the sea are the Tapti, Narmada, Mandovi and Zuari.  Vegetation is mostly deciduous, but the Malabar Coast moist forests constitute a unique ecoregion.  The Western Coastal Plain can be divided into two parts, the Konkan and the Malabar Coast.  === Islands === The Lakshadweep and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands are India's two major island formations and are classified as union territories.  The Lakshadweep Islands lie 200 to 440 km (120 to 270 mi) off the coast of Kerala in the Arabian sea with an area of 32 km2 (12 sq mi)"
    },
