      "emoji": "",
      "question": "These models predict an upward trend in the global mean surface temperature, with the most rapid increase in temperature being projected for the higher latitudes of the _____",
      "answer": "Northern Hemisphere",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Global climate models can be dynamically or statistically downscaled to regional climate models to analyze impacts of climate change on a local scale.  Examples are ICON or mechanistically downscaled data such as CHELSA (Climatologies at high resolution for the earth's land surface areas).  The most talked-about applications of these models in recent years have been their use to infer the consequences of increasing greenhouse gases in the atmosphere, primarily carbon dioxide (see greenhouse gas).  These models predict an upward trend in the global mean surface temperature, with the most rapid increase in temperature being projected for the higher latitudes of the Northern Hemisphere.  Models can range from relatively simple to quite complex.  Simple radiant heat transfer models treat the Earth as a single point and average outgoing energy.  This can be expanded vertically (as in radiative-convective models), or horizontally"
    },
