      "answer": "Light Combat Helicopter",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In recent times, India has also manufactured its own aircraft, such as the HAL Tejas, a 4th-generation fighter, and the HAL Dhruv, a multi-role helicopter, which has been exported to several countries, including Israel, Burma, Nepal, and Ecuador.  A weaponised version of Dhruv is called the HAL Rudra, which is armed with a high-velocity M621 20 mm cannon, long-range 70 mm rockets (8 km), air-to-air missiles (Mistral-II), and MAWS (missile approach warning system).  Outcome of Kargil War highlighted the requirement of an attack helicopter specially made for such high-altitude operations.  The HAL Light Combat Helicopter (LCH) is a multi-role combat helicopter being developed in India by Hindustan Aeronautics Limited (HAL) for use by the Indian Army and the Indian Air Force.  The LCH is being designed to fit into an anti-infantry and anti-armour role and will be able to operate at high altitudes.  LCH will be fitted with indigenous anti-tank missile Helina.  India also maintains unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) squadrons (primarily Searcher-II and Heron from Israel) which can be used to carry out ground and aerial surveillance"
