      "fact": " In the African and Middle East campaigns, captured Indian troops were given a choice to join the German Army, to eventually \"liberate\" India from Britain, instead of being sent to POW camps.  These men, along with Indian students who were in Germany when the war broke out, made up what was called the Free India Legion.  They were originally intended as pathfinders for German forces in Asia but were soon sent to help guard the Atlantic Wall.  Few who were part of the Free India Legion ever saw any combat, and very few were ever stationed outside Europe.  At its height, the Free India Legion had over 3,000 troops in its ranks.  Indian POWs also joined the Indian National Army, which was allied with the Empire of Japan.  It was raised by a former colonel of the Indian Army, General Mohan Singh, but was later led by Subhas Chandra Bose and Rash Bihari Bose"
