      "question": "21 per cent of the Navy's officer cadre and 47 per cent of its sailors opted to join the portion of the fleet which became the _____",
      "answer": "Royal Pakistan Navy",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The strike ultimately failed as the sailors did not receive substantial support from either the Indian Army or from political leaders in Congress or the Muslim League.  Choudhry and Bhaskar Sadashiv Soman, both of whom would eventually command the Pakistani and Indian Navies, respectively, became the first Indian RIN officers to attain the acting rank of captain.  === Independence to the end of the 20th century === Following independence and the partition of India on 15 August 1947, the RIN's depleted fleet of ships and remaining personnel were divided between the newly independent Dominion of India and Dominion of Pakistan.  21 per cent of the Navy's officer cadre and 47 per cent of its sailors opted to join the portion of the fleet which became the Royal Pakistan Navy.  The Indian share of the Navy consisted of 32 vessels along with 11,000 personnel.  Effective from the same date, all British officers were compulsorily retired from the Navy and its reserve components, with Indian officers being promoted to replace British senior officers.  However, a number of British flag and senior officers were invited to continue serving in the RIN, as only nine of the Navy's Indian commissi"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2825",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Defence",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:01:27.234Z",
      "subject": "Defence",
      "subSubject": "Defence",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ Savignac Hall headed the Navy as its first Commander-in-Chief (C-in-C) post-Independence",
      "answer": "Rear Admiral John Talbot",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The Indian share of the Navy consisted of 32 vessels along with 11,000 personnel.  Effective from the same date, all British officers were compulsorily retired from the Navy and its reserve components, with Indian officers being promoted to replace British senior officers.  However, a number of British flag and senior officers were invited to continue serving in the RIN, as only nine of the Navy's Indian commissioned officers had more than 10 years' service, with the majority of them only having served from five to eight years.  Rear Admiral John Talbot Savignac Hall headed the Navy as its first Commander-in-Chief (C-in-C) post-Independence.  Mukherji, the first Indian officer in the RIN, became the first Indian to be promoted acting engineer captain.  In May 1948, Captain Ajitendu Chakraverti became the first Indian officer to be appointed to the rank of commodore.  When India became a republic on 26 January 1950, the Royal prefix was dropped and the name Indian Navy was officially adopted"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2826",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Defence",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:01:27.234Z",
      "subject": "Defence",
      "subSubject": "Defence",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ 1948, Captain Ajitendu Chakraverti became the first Indian officer to be appointed to the rank of commodore",
      "answer": "In May",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " However, a number of British flag and senior officers were invited to continue serving in the RIN, as only nine of the Navy's Indian commissioned officers had more than 10 years' service, with the majority of them only having served from five to eight years.  Rear Admiral John Talbot Savignac Hall headed the Navy as its first Commander-in-Chief (C-in-C) post-Independence.  Mukherji, the first Indian officer in the RIN, became the first Indian to be promoted acting engineer captain.  In May 1948, Captain Ajitendu Chakraverti became the first Indian officer to be appointed to the rank of commodore.  When India became a republic on 26 January 1950, the Royal prefix was dropped and the name Indian Navy was officially adopted.  The prefix for naval vessels was changed from His Majesty's Indian Ship (HMIS) to Indian Naval Ship (INS).  At the same time, the imperial crown in insignia was replaced with the Lion Capital of Ashoka and the Union Jack in the canton of the White Ensign was replaced with the Indian Tricolour"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2827",
      "type": "fill_blank",
