      "question": "At the same time, the imperial crown in insignia was replaced with the _____ of Ashoka and the Union Jack in the canton of the White Ensign was replaced with the Indian Tricolour",
      "answer": "Lion Capital",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In May 1948, Captain Ajitendu Chakraverti became the first Indian officer to be appointed to the rank of commodore.  When India became a republic on 26 January 1950, the Royal prefix was dropped and the name Indian Navy was officially adopted.  The prefix for naval vessels was changed from His Majesty's Indian Ship (HMIS) to Indian Naval Ship (INS).  At the same time, the imperial crown in insignia was replaced with the Lion Capital of Ashoka and the Union Jack in the canton of the White Ensign was replaced with the Indian Tricolour.  By 1955, the Navy had largely overcome its post-Independence personnel shortfalls.  During the early years following independence, many British officers continued to serve in the Navy on secondment from the Royal Navy, due to the post-Independence retirement or transfer of many experienced officers to the Royal or the Pakistan navies.  The first C-in-C of the Navy was Admiral Sir Edward Parry who took over from Hall in 1948 and handed over to Admiral Sir Charles Thomas Mark Pizey in 1951"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2829",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Defence",
