      "answer": "Turkmenistan Uzbekistan India",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " India's military presence and activities have been significant, beginning with India's extensive support of the anti-Taliban Afghan Northern Alliance (ANA).  India began renovating the Farkhor Air Base and stationed aircraft of the Indian Air Force there.  The Farkhor Air Base became fully operational in 2006, and 12 MiG-29 bombers and trainer aircraft are planned to be stationed there.  Turkmenistan Uzbekistan India has an embassy in Tashkent.  Uzbekistan has an embassy in New Delhi.  Uzbekistan has had a great impact on Indian culture mostly due to the Mughal Empire which was founded by Babur of Ferghana (in present-day Uzbekistan) who created his empire southward first in Afghanistan and then in India.  === East === China Despite lingering suspicions remaining from the 1962 Sino-Indian War, the 1967 Nathu La and Cho La incidents, and continuing boundary disputes over Aksai Chin and Arunachal Pradesh, Sino-Indian relations have improved gradually since 1988"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3304",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.895Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ 1996, Chinese President Jiang Zemin visited India during a tour of South Asia",
      "answer": "In December",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " === East === China Despite lingering suspicions remaining from the 1962 Sino-Indian War, the 1967 Nathu La and Cho La incidents, and continuing boundary disputes over Aksai Chin and Arunachal Pradesh, Sino-Indian relations have improved gradually since 1988.  Both countries have sought to reduce tensions along the frontier, expand trade and cultural ties, and normalise relations.  A series of high-level visits between the two nations have helped improve relations.  In December 1996, Chinese President Jiang Zemin visited India during a tour of South Asia.  While in New Delhi, he signed with the Indian prime minister a series of confidence-building measures for the disputed borders.  Sino-Indian relations suffered a brief setback in May 1998 when the Indian Defence minister justified the country's nuclear tests by citing potential threats from China.  However, in June 1999, during the Kargil crisis, then-External Affairs Minister Jaswant Singh visited Beijing and stated that India did not consider China a threat"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3305",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.896Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "While in _____, he signed with the Indian prime minister a series of confidence-building measures for the disputed borders",
      "answer": "New Delhi",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Both countries have sought to reduce tensions along the frontier, expand trade and cultural ties, and normalise relations.  A series of high-level visits between the two nations have helped improve relations.  In December 1996, Chinese President Jiang Zemin visited India during a tour of South Asia.  While in New Delhi, he signed with the Indian prime minister a series of confidence-building measures for the disputed borders.  Sino-Indian relations suffered a brief setback in May 1998 when the Indian Defence minister justified the country's nuclear tests by citing potential threats from China.  However, in June 1999, during the Kargil crisis, then-External Affairs Minister Jaswant Singh visited Beijing and stated that India did not consider China a threat.  By 2001, relations between India and China were on the mend, and the two sides handled the move from Tibet to India of the 17th Karmapa in January 2000 with delicacy and tact"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3306",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.897Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Sino-Indian relations suffered a brief setback in May 1998 when the _____ minister justified the country's nuclear tests by citing potential threats from China",
      "answer": "Indian Defence",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " A series of high-level visits between the two nations have helped improve relations.  In December 1996, Chinese President Jiang Zemin visited India during a tour of South Asia.  While in New Delhi, he signed with the Indian prime minister a series of confidence-building measures for the disputed borders.  Sino-Indian relations suffered a brief setback in May 1998 when the Indian Defence minister justified the country's nuclear tests by citing potential threats from China.  However, in June 1999, during the Kargil crisis, then-External Affairs Minister Jaswant Singh visited Beijing and stated that India did not consider China a threat.  By 2001, relations between India and China were on the mend, and the two sides handled the move from Tibet to India of the 17th Karmapa in January 2000 with delicacy and tact.  In 2003, India formally recognised Tibet as a part of China, and China recognised Sikkim as a formal part of India in 2004"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3307",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.898Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "However, in June 1999, during the Kargil crisis, then-_____ Singh visited Beijing and stated that India did not consider China a threat",
      "answer": "External Affairs Minister Jaswant",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In December 1996, Chinese President Jiang Zemin visited India during a tour of South Asia.  While in New Delhi, he signed with the Indian prime minister a series of confidence-building measures for the disputed borders.  Sino-Indian relations suffered a brief setback in May 1998 when the Indian Defence minister justified the country's nuclear tests by citing potential threats from China.  However, in June 1999, during the Kargil crisis, then-External Affairs Minister Jaswant Singh visited Beijing and stated that India did not consider China a threat.  By 2001, relations between India and China were on the mend, and the two sides handled the move from Tibet to India of the 17th Karmapa in January 2000 with delicacy and tact.  In 2003, India formally recognised Tibet as a part of China, and China recognised Sikkim as a formal part of India in 2004. Since 2004, the economic rise of both China and India has also helped forge closer relations between the two"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3308",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.899Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Relations were lost due to _____ skirmishes and its progress",
      "answer": "Galwan Valley",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " However, China's economic interests have clashed with those of India.  Both countries are the largest Asian investors in Africa and have competed for control over their large natural resources.  There was a tense situation due to the soldiers' stand-off in Doklam, Bhutan; but that was resolved early.  Relations were lost due to Galwan Valley skirmishes and its progress.  India ceased imports of Chinese products.  Various measures were taken, such as several contracts with the Chinese companies involved in railways, networks and several items productions, which were cancelled in response.  The outbreak of the Coronavirus pandemic from Wuhan also hampered the relations"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3309",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.899Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____-Japan relations have always been strong",
      "answer": "Japan India",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In mid-January 2021, it was reported that both countries had finally agreed upon the de-escalation from their positions.  Footage of Chinese troops removing tents/barracks was released.  Both countries also agreed that India would move back to Finger-3, while China retained its position back to Finger-8, and also declared the area from Finger-3 to Finger-8 to be \"No man's land\".  Japan India-Japan relations have always been strong.  India has culturally influenced Japan through Buddhism.  During World War II, the Imperial Japanese Army helped Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose's Indian National Army.  Relations have remained warm since India's independence, despite Japan imposing sanctions on India after the 1998 Pokhran-II nuclear tests (the sanctions were removed in 2001)"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3310",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.900Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ II, the Imperial Japanese Army helped Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose's Indian National Army",
      "answer": "During World War",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Both countries also agreed that India would move back to Finger-3, while China retained its position back to Finger-8, and also declared the area from Finger-3 to Finger-8 to be \"No man's land\".  Japan India-Japan relations have always been strong.  India has culturally influenced Japan through Buddhism.  During World War II, the Imperial Japanese Army helped Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose's Indian National Army.  Relations have remained warm since India's independence, despite Japan imposing sanctions on India after the 1998 Pokhran-II nuclear tests (the sanctions were removed in 2001).  Japanese companies, like Sony, Toyota, and Honda, have manufacturing facilities in India, and with the growth of the Indian economy, India is a big market for Japanese firms.  The most prominent Japanese company to have a big investment in India is automobiles giant Suzuki which is in partnership with Indian automobile company Maruti Suzuki, the largest car manufacturer in India"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3311",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.901Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Honda was also a partner in \"_____\", one of the largest motorcycle sellers in the world (the companies split in 2011)",
      "answer": "Hero Honda",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Relations have remained warm since India's independence, despite Japan imposing sanctions on India after the 1998 Pokhran-II nuclear tests (the sanctions were removed in 2001).  Japanese companies, like Sony, Toyota, and Honda, have manufacturing facilities in India, and with the growth of the Indian economy, India is a big market for Japanese firms.  The most prominent Japanese company to have a big investment in India is automobiles giant Suzuki which is in partnership with Indian automobile company Maruti Suzuki, the largest car manufacturer in India.  Honda was also a partner in \"Hero Honda\", one of the largest motorcycle sellers in the world (the companies split in 2011).  According to Former Prime Minister Shinzō Abe's arc of freedom theory, it is in Japan's interests to develop closer ties with India, the world's most populous democracy, while its relations with China remain chilly.  To this end, Japan has funded many infrastructure projects in India, most notably in New Delhi's metro subway system.  In December 2006, then Prime Minister Manmohan Singh's visit to Japan culminated in the signing of the \"Joint Statement Towards Japan-India Strategic and Global Partnership\""
    },
    {
      "id": "q3312",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.902Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "To this end, Japan has funded many infrastructure projects in India, most notably in _____'s metro subway system",
      "answer": "New Delhi",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The most prominent Japanese company to have a big investment in India is automobiles giant Suzuki which is in partnership with Indian automobile company Maruti Suzuki, the largest car manufacturer in India.  Honda was also a partner in \"Hero Honda\", one of the largest motorcycle sellers in the world (the companies split in 2011).  According to Former Prime Minister Shinzō Abe's arc of freedom theory, it is in Japan's interests to develop closer ties with India, the world's most populous democracy, while its relations with China remain chilly.  To this end, Japan has funded many infrastructure projects in India, most notably in New Delhi's metro subway system.  In December 2006, then Prime Minister Manmohan Singh's visit to Japan culminated in the signing of the \"Joint Statement Towards Japan-India Strategic and Global Partnership\".  Indian applicants were welcomed in 2006 to the JET Programme, starting with just one slot available in 2006 and 41 in 2007.  Also, in 2007, the Japan Self-Defense Forces took part in a naval exercise in the Indian Ocean, known as Malabar 2007, which also involved the naval forces of India, Australia, Singapore and the United States"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3313",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.903Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ 2006, then Prime Minister Manmohan Singh's visit to Japan culminated in the signing of the \"Joint Statement Towards Japan-India Strategic and Global Partnership\"",
      "answer": "In December",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Honda was also a partner in \"Hero Honda\", one of the largest motorcycle sellers in the world (the companies split in 2011).  According to Former Prime Minister Shinzō Abe's arc of freedom theory, it is in Japan's interests to develop closer ties with India, the world's most populous democracy, while its relations with China remain chilly.  To this end, Japan has funded many infrastructure projects in India, most notably in New Delhi's metro subway system.  In December 2006, then Prime Minister Manmohan Singh's visit to Japan culminated in the signing of the \"Joint Statement Towards Japan-India Strategic and Global Partnership\".  Indian applicants were welcomed in 2006 to the JET Programme, starting with just one slot available in 2006 and 41 in 2007.  Also, in 2007, the Japan Self-Defense Forces took part in a naval exercise in the Indian Ocean, known as Malabar 2007, which also involved the naval forces of India, Australia, Singapore and the United States.  In October 2008, Japan signed an agreement with India under which it would grant the latter a low-interest loan worth US$4"
    },
