      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.961Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ relations between Sri Lanka and India have enjoyed historically a good relationship",
      "answer": "Sri Lanka Bilateral",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " India blamed Pakistan which was denied by the Pakistani establishment.  India retaliated with an airstrike on Balakot, a region claimed and controlled by Pakistan.  A new chapter in peace was ignited when it was suddenly declared that a back-door peace settlement over ceasing the cross-border firing across LOC was signed between the armies of both sides, and a steady growth in the countries coming together was observed.  Sri Lanka Bilateral relations between Sri Lanka and India have enjoyed historically a good relationship.  The two countries share near-identical racial and cultural ties.  According to traditional Sri Lankan chronicles (Dipavamsa), Buddhism was introduced into Sri Lanka in the 4th century BCE by Venerable Mahinda, the son of Indian Emperor Ashoka, during the reign of Sri Lanka's King Devanampiya Tissa.  During this time, a sapling of the Bodhi Tree was brought to Sri Lanka and the first monasteries and Buddhist monuments were established"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3364",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.962Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "During this time, a sapling of the _____ was brought to Sri Lanka and the first monasteries and Buddhist monuments were established",
      "answer": "Bodhi Tree",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Sri Lanka Bilateral relations between Sri Lanka and India have enjoyed historically a good relationship.  The two countries share near-identical racial and cultural ties.  According to traditional Sri Lankan chronicles (Dipavamsa), Buddhism was introduced into Sri Lanka in the 4th century BCE by Venerable Mahinda, the son of Indian Emperor Ashoka, during the reign of Sri Lanka's King Devanampiya Tissa.  During this time, a sapling of the Bodhi Tree was brought to Sri Lanka and the first monasteries and Buddhist monuments were established. Nevertheless, relations post-independence were affected by the Sri Lankan Civil War and by the failure of Indian intervention during the civil war as well as India's support for Tamil Tiger militants.  India is Sri Lanka's only neighbour, separated by the Palk Strait; both nations occupy a strategic position in South Asia and have sought to build a common security umbrella in the Indian Ocean.  India-Sri Lanka relations have undergone a qualitative and quantitative transformation in the recent past"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3365",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.964Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "India-_____ relations have undergone a qualitative and quantitative transformation in the recent past",
      "answer": "Sri Lanka",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " During this time, a sapling of the Bodhi Tree was brought to Sri Lanka and the first monasteries and Buddhist monuments were established. Nevertheless, relations post-independence were affected by the Sri Lankan Civil War and by the failure of Indian intervention during the civil war as well as India's support for Tamil Tiger militants.  India is Sri Lanka's only neighbour, separated by the Palk Strait; both nations occupy a strategic position in South Asia and have sought to build a common security umbrella in the Indian Ocean.  India-Sri Lanka relations have undergone a qualitative and quantitative transformation in the recent past.  Political relations are close, trade and investments have increased dramatically, infrastructural linkages are constantly being augmented, defence collaboration has increased and there is a general, broad-based improvement across all sectors of bilateral cooperation.  India was the first country to respond to Sri Lanka's request for assistance after the tsunami in December 2004.  In July 2006, India evacuated 430 Sri Lankan nationals from Lebanon, first to Cyprus by Indian Navy ships and then to Delhi and Colombo by special Air India flights"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3366",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.966Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "India was the first country to respond to _____'s request for assistance after the tsunami in December 2004",
      "answer": "Sri Lanka",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " India is Sri Lanka's only neighbour, separated by the Palk Strait; both nations occupy a strategic position in South Asia and have sought to build a common security umbrella in the Indian Ocean.  India-Sri Lanka relations have undergone a qualitative and quantitative transformation in the recent past.  Political relations are close, trade and investments have increased dramatically, infrastructural linkages are constantly being augmented, defence collaboration has increased and there is a general, broad-based improvement across all sectors of bilateral cooperation.  India was the first country to respond to Sri Lanka's request for assistance after the tsunami in December 2004.  In July 2006, India evacuated 430 Sri Lankan nationals from Lebanon, first to Cyprus by Indian Navy ships and then to Delhi and Colombo by special Air India flights.  There exists a broad consensus within the Sri Lankan polity on the primacy of India in Sri Lanka's external relations matrix.  Both the major political parties in Sri Lanka, the Sri Lanka Freedom Party and the United Nationalist Party have contributed to the rapid development of bilateral relations in the last ten years"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3367",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:20.969Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ 2006, India evacuated 430 Sri Lankan nationals from Lebanon, first to Cyprus by Indian Navy ships and then to Delhi and Colombo by special Air India flights",
      "answer": "In July",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " India-Sri Lanka relations have undergone a qualitative and quantitative transformation in the recent past.  Political relations are close, trade and investments have increased dramatically, infrastructural linkages are constantly being augmented, defence collaboration has increased and there is a general, broad-based improvement across all sectors of bilateral cooperation.  India was the first country to respond to Sri Lanka's request for assistance after the tsunami in December 2004.  In July 2006, India evacuated 430 Sri Lankan nationals from Lebanon, first to Cyprus by Indian Navy ships and then to Delhi and Colombo by special Air India flights.  There exists a broad consensus within the Sri Lankan polity on the primacy of India in Sri Lanka's external relations matrix.  Both the major political parties in Sri Lanka, the Sri Lanka Freedom Party and the United Nationalist Party have contributed to the rapid development of bilateral relations in the last ten years.  Sri Lanka has supported India's candidature to the permanent membership of the UN Security Council"
    },
