      "id": "q3403",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:21.015Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "International Relations",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "India's economic liberalisation and its \"_____\" policy have led to a major expansion in bilateral trade, which grew from US$2",
      "answer": "Look East",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Singapore is the 8th largest source of investment in India and the largest among ASEAN member nations.  It is also India's 9th biggest trading partner as of 2005–06.  Its cumulative investment in India totals US$3 billion as of 2006 and is expected to rise to US$5 billion by 2010 and US$10 billion by 2015.  India's economic liberalisation and its \"Look East\" policy have led to a major expansion in bilateral trade, which grew from US$2. 2 billion in 2001 to US$9–10 billion in 2006 – a 400% growth in five years – and to US$50 billion by 2010.  Singapore accounts for 38% of India's trade with ASEAN member nations and 3. 4% of its total foreign trade"
    },
