      "answer": "Indian Armed Forces",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Substantial trade with the former Soviet Union plummeted after the Soviet collapse and has yet to recover.  Longstanding military supply relationships were similarly disrupted due to questions over financing, although Russia continues to be India's largest supplier of military systems and spare parts.  The relationship with USSR was tested (and proven) during the 1971 war with Pakistan, which led to the subsequent liberation of Bangladesh.  Soon after the victory of the Indian Armed Forces, one of the foreign delegates to visit India was Admiral S.  Gorshkov, Chief of the Soviet Navy.  During his visit to Mumbai (Bombay), he came on board INS Vikrant.  During a conversation with Vice Admiral Swaraj Prakash, Gorshkov asked the Vice Admiral, \"Were you worried about a battle against the American carrier?\" He answered himself: \"Well, you had no reason to be worried, as I had a Soviet nuclear submarine trailing the American task force all the way into the Indian Ocean"
