      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.176Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Union of India case, the _____ ruled that the amendment to Article 31C was not valid and ultra vires",
      "answer": "Supreme Court",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In 2007, the Supreme Court ruled that there could not be any blanket immunity from judicial review for the laws inserted in the Ninth Schedule.  Apex court also stated it shall examine laws included in the Ninth Schedule after 1973 for any incompatibility with the basic structure doctrine.  === Amendment to Article 31C === Section 4 of the 42nd Amendment, had changed Article 31C of the Constitution to accord precedence to the Directive Principles (earlier applicable only to clauses b & c of Article 39) over the fundamental rights of individuals.  Union of India case, the Supreme Court ruled that the amendment to Article 31C was not valid and ultra vires.  === Right to property === The Constitution originally provided for the right to property under Articles 19 and 31.  Article 19 guaranteed to all citizens the right to acquire, hold and dispose of property.  Article 31 provided that \"no person shall be deprived of his property save by authority of law"
    },
