      "question": "According to _____, Indian courts had 27% rise in backlog between December 2019 and April 2022",
      "answer": "National Judicial Data Grid",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Judiciary issues have been depicted in several films, such as Court.  === Backlog === The Indian judiciary is overburdened far beyond capacity with a backlog of cases.  Nearly 1,82,000 cases have been pending for over 30 years.  According to National Judicial Data Grid, Indian courts had 27% rise in backlog between December 2019 and April 2022.  In 2024 India's ratio of judges to population is 21 per million people, which is one of the lowest in the world.  In 2002, the Ministry of Law and Justice set a goal of increasing that number to 50.  For comparison, the United States as 120"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3896",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.212Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "On 12 January 2012, the _____ said that confidence in the judiciary was decreasing, posing a threat to the country",
      "answer": "Supreme Court",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Jagdev claimed that the judiciary does not attract the best legal talent in part because of disparity in compensation.  In recent years scandals have besmirched the judiciary's reputation.  The District judiciary works in appalling conditions.  On 12 January 2012, the Supreme Court said that confidence in the judiciary was decreasing, posing a threat to the country.  It acknowledged the problems of vacancies in trial courts, unwillingness of lawyers to become judges, and the failure of the apex judiciary in filling vacant HC posts.  One proposal is that access to justice must be made a Constitutional right requiring the executive to provide the necessary infrastructure for protecting that right.  The Court also wanted the Government of India to detail the work being done by the National Mission for Justice Delivery and Legal Reforms"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3897",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.213Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "According to _____- India 2015, 67",
      "answer": "Prison Statistics",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The Court also wanted the Government of India to detail the work being done by the National Mission for Justice Delivery and Legal Reforms.  Undertrials outnumber convicts in the prison population.  Ordinary citizens have been imprisoned for espionage for overstaying their visa or straying across international borders, languishing in prison for years due to the slow redressal process.  According to Prison Statistics- India 2015, 67. 2% of the total prison population of India is under trial, which means they have not yet(by 2015) been convicted by the court.  To reduce pendency, 'Fast-track courts', 'Evening courts/Morning courts' were set up and met with mixed success.  Mobile courts were set up to bring 'justice at the doorsteps' of litigants of judge-poor rural areas"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3898",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.213Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ is an informal, alternative mechanism that has been a success in tackling backlogs, especially in pre-litigation matters, settling cases before they enter the courts",
      "answer": "Lok Adalats",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": "2% of the total prison population of India is under trial, which means they have not yet(by 2015) been convicted by the court.  To reduce pendency, 'Fast-track courts', 'Evening courts/Morning courts' were set up and met with mixed success.  Mobile courts were set up to bring 'justice at the doorsteps' of litigants of judge-poor rural areas.  Lok Adalats is an informal, alternative mechanism that has been a success in tackling backlogs, especially in pre-litigation matters, settling cases before they enter the courts.  According to a report released by Centre for Public Policy Research and British Deputy High Commission \"a total of 16,884 commercial disputes [are] pending in High Courts with original jurisdiction.  Of these Madras High court tops with 5,865.  With the number of commercial disputes growing rapidly, facilitating a seamless dispute resolution system through alternate means has become crucial"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3899",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.214Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Of these _____ court tops with 5,865",
      "answer": "Madras High",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Mobile courts were set up to bring 'justice at the doorsteps' of litigants of judge-poor rural areas.  Lok Adalats is an informal, alternative mechanism that has been a success in tackling backlogs, especially in pre-litigation matters, settling cases before they enter the courts.  According to a report released by Centre for Public Policy Research and British Deputy High Commission \"a total of 16,884 commercial disputes [are] pending in High Courts with original jurisdiction.  Of these Madras High court tops with 5,865.  With the number of commercial disputes growing rapidly, facilitating a seamless dispute resolution system through alternate means has become crucial. \" Economists Boehm and Oberfeld calculated that the backlog costs the Indian economy several percentage points of GDP.  === Judicial corruption === According to Transparency International, judicial corruption in India is attributable to factors such as \"delays in the disposal of cases, shortage of judges and complex procedures, all of which are exacerbated by a preponderance of new laws\""
    },
    {
      "id": "q3900",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.215Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "\" _____ and Oberfeld calculated that the backlog costs the Indian economy several percentage points of GDP",
      "answer": "Economists Boehm",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " According to a report released by Centre for Public Policy Research and British Deputy High Commission \"a total of 16,884 commercial disputes [are] pending in High Courts with original jurisdiction.  Of these Madras High court tops with 5,865.  With the number of commercial disputes growing rapidly, facilitating a seamless dispute resolution system through alternate means has become crucial. \" Economists Boehm and Oberfeld calculated that the backlog costs the Indian economy several percentage points of GDP.  === Judicial corruption === According to Transparency International, judicial corruption in India is attributable to factors such as \"delays in the disposal of cases, shortage of judges and complex procedures, all of which are exacerbated by a preponderance of new laws\".  Corruption has reached the Supreme Court.  Notable cases include: In December 2009, legal activist and lawyer Prashant Bhushan stated in court, \"out of the last 16 to 17 Chief Justices, half have been corrupt\" In November 2010, former Law Minister, Shanti Bhushan echoed Prashant Bhushan's claim"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3901",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.216Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "In 2011, _____, former judge at the Calcutta High Court became the first judge in the India to be impeached by the Rajya Sabha for misappropriation of funds",
      "answer": "Soumitra Sen",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Corruption has reached the Supreme Court.  Notable cases include: In December 2009, legal activist and lawyer Prashant Bhushan stated in court, \"out of the last 16 to 17 Chief Justices, half have been corrupt\" In November 2010, former Law Minister, Shanti Bhushan echoed Prashant Bhushan's claim.  There have been allegations that judges with doubtful integrity were elevated within the higher judiciary and campaigns held for their impeachment.  In 2011, Soumitra Sen, former judge at the Calcutta High Court became the first judge in the India to be impeached by the Rajya Sabha for misappropriation of funds.  However, the impeachment proceedings were dropped after Sen choose to resign.  In November 2011, a former Supreme Court Justice Ruma Pal slammed the higher judiciary for what she called the seven sins.  She listed the sins as: Turning a blind eye to the injudicious conduct of a colleague"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3902",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.216Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ 2011, a former Supreme Court Justice Ruma Pal slammed the higher judiciary for what she called the seven sins",
      "answer": "In November",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " There have been allegations that judges with doubtful integrity were elevated within the higher judiciary and campaigns held for their impeachment.  In 2011, Soumitra Sen, former judge at the Calcutta High Court became the first judge in the India to be impeached by the Rajya Sabha for misappropriation of funds.  However, the impeachment proceedings were dropped after Sen choose to resign.  In November 2011, a former Supreme Court Justice Ruma Pal slammed the higher judiciary for what she called the seven sins.  She listed the sins as: Turning a blind eye to the injudicious conduct of a colleague.  Hypocrisy – the distortion of the norm of judicial independence.  Secrecy – lack of transparency in the appointment of judges to the High and Supreme Court"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3903",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.216Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Secrecy – lack of transparency in the appointment of judges to the High and _____",
      "answer": "Supreme Court",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In November 2011, a former Supreme Court Justice Ruma Pal slammed the higher judiciary for what she called the seven sins.  She listed the sins as: Turning a blind eye to the injudicious conduct of a colleague.  Hypocrisy – the distortion of the norm of judicial independence.  Secrecy – lack of transparency in the appointment of judges to the High and Supreme Court.  Plagiarism and prolixity – SC judges often lift whole passages from earlier decisions without acknowledgement – and use long-winded, verbose language.  Arrogance – the higher judiciary has claimed superiority and independence to mask their own indiscipline and transgression of norms and procedures.  Professional incompetence – judges arrive at decisions of grave importance ignoring precedent or judicial principle"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3904",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.217Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Despite the demands to have him removed and suspended, Varma was transferred to the _____, sparking a backlash from lawyers and other judges",
      "answer": "Allahabad High Court",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Nepotism – wherein favors are sought and dispensed by some judges.  In March 2025, large bundles of cash, some burnt, were found in the house of Delhi High Court Judge Yashwant Varma by the fire department, after a fire was reported at his house, reigniting a major controversy on judicial corruption.  The Supreme Court declined to hear a plea seeking an FIR against Varma following the expose.  Despite the demands to have him removed and suspended, Varma was transferred to the Allahabad High Court, sparking a backlash from lawyers and other judges.  An impeachment process against Verma is due to be initiated in the Parliament monsoon season in July 2025.  On 26 February 2026, the Supreme Court slammed the NCERT, after a class 8 social science textbook revealed a section on corruption in judiciary.  Calling it a deep-rooted conspiracy to defame and undermine the judiciary, the Chief Justice Surya Kant, in a suo motu case hearing, called the move to teach pupils about judicial corruption and pendency of cases as reckless, and ordered that the books should not be issued to students"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3905",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:02:33.217Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Constitution",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "On 26 February 2026, the _____ slammed the NCERT, after a class 8 social science textbook revealed a section on corruption in judiciary",
      "answer": "Supreme Court",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The Supreme Court declined to hear a plea seeking an FIR against Varma following the expose.  Despite the demands to have him removed and suspended, Varma was transferred to the Allahabad High Court, sparking a backlash from lawyers and other judges.  An impeachment process against Verma is due to be initiated in the Parliament monsoon season in July 2025.  On 26 February 2026, the Supreme Court slammed the NCERT, after a class 8 social science textbook revealed a section on corruption in judiciary.  Calling it a deep-rooted conspiracy to defame and undermine the judiciary, the Chief Justice Surya Kant, in a suo motu case hearing, called the move to teach pupils about judicial corruption and pendency of cases as reckless, and ordered that the books should not be issued to students.  === Nepotism === Uncle judge syndrome is a nepotistic phenomenon in the Indian judiciary where the collegium system, the opaque system of which the incumbent judges of the Supreme Court of India appoint new judges, is misused to appoint family and relatives of the judges.  In 2017, the Supreme Court had recognised the rot of the collegium, but as of 2025 little has been done to fix it"
    },
