      "answer": "South Asia",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Barth identifies the origin of the stratification from the historical segregation between pak (pure) and paleed (impure)—defined by the family's social or religious status, occupation and involvement in sexual crimes.  There is history of skin color defining Pak/Paleed, but that does not have historical roots, and was adopted by outsiders through analogy with the Hindu Caste system.  Endogamy is very common in the form of arranged consanguineous marriages among Muslims in India and Pakistan.  Malik states that the lack of religious sanction makes qaum a \"quasi-caste\", and something that is found in Islam outside South Asia as well.  === Sikh === The Sikh literature mentions Varna as Varan, and Jati as Zat or Zat-biradari.  Eleanor Nesbitt, a professor of Religion and author of books on Sikhism, states that the Varan is described as a class system, while Zat has some caste system features in Sikh literature.  In theory, Sikh literature does not recognise caste hierarchy or differences, but, in practice, states Nesbitt, widespread endogamy practices among Sikhs have been prevalent in modern times, and poorer Sikhs of disadvantaged castes continue to gather in their own places of wor"
    },
