      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:03:34.197Z",
      "subject": "Society",
      "subSubject": "Society",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "\" === Colonial period and English education === In _____, the English Education Act was passed by the colonial government, making English the formal medium of education in all scho",
      "answer": "1835",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In 1818, the fall of Maratha Empire lead to large parts of India coming under British rule.  During the decade of 1820–30, detailed surveys of the indigenous education system that were prevalent in their provinces were conducted by the British.  Prendergast, a member of the Governor's Council in Bombay Presidency, recorded the following about indigenous schools on 27 June 1821: \"I need hardly mention what every member of the Board knows as well as I do, that there is hardly a village, great or small, throughout our territories, in which there is not at least one school, and in larger villages more; many in every town, and in large cities in every division; where young natives are taught reading, writing and arithmetic, upon a system so economical, from a handful or two of grain, to perhaps a rupee per month to the school master, according to the ability of the parents, and at the same time so simple and effectual, that there is hardly a cultivator or petty dealer who is not competent to keep his own accounts with a degree of accuracy, in my opinion, beyond what we meet with amongst the lower orders in our own country. \" === Colonial period and English education === In 1835, the En"
    },
    {
      "id": "q4179",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Society",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:03:34.197Z",
      "subject": "Society",
