      "answer": "Dadasaheb Phalke",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Phalke is widely considered the father of Indian cinema.  The Dadasaheb Phalke Award is India's highest award in cinema, given annually by the Government of India for lifetime contribution to Indian cinema.  The Marathi film industry, initially located in Kolhapur, has spread throughout Mumbai.  Well known for its art films, the early Marathi film industry included acclaimed directors such as Dadasaheb Phalke, V.  Shantaram, Raja Thakur, Bhalji Pendharkar, Pralhad Keshav Atre, Baburao Painter, and Dada Kondke.  Some of the directors who made acclaimed films in Marathi are Jabbar Patel, Mahesh Manjrekar, Amol Palekar, and Sanjay Surkar.  Durga Khote was one of the first women from respectable families to enter the film industry, thus breaking a social taboo"
