      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Ashokan Inscriptions of 3rd cenctury BC at Dhauli and Jaugarh is the ancient unchanged evidence of Odia language.  Odia language literary history started with the Kharavel's Hatigumpha inscription (40 B. ) was the real evidence of past Odia cultural, political, ritual and social status and it is the first poetic stake inscription.  Though Ashoka created many rock edicts and inscriptions before Kharavela, his instructions for administration were written in a rude and chocked language.  On the other hand, the Hatigumpha inscriptions show the flexibility of a language in a sweet flow.  The buddhist charyapadas written in the 7th century CE on the period of Bhauma-Kara dynasty .  Odia has a rich literary heritage, the medieval period dating back to the 13th century"
