      "answer": "Michael Angelo",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In May 1926 Tagore reached Naples; the next day he met Mussolini in Rome.  Their warm rapport ended when Tagore felt compelled to denounce Fascism in an article published in The Manchester Guardian.  He had earlier enthused: \"[w]without any doubt he is a great personality.  There is such a massive vigor in that head that it reminds one of Michael Angelo's chisel. \" A \"fire-bath\" of fascism was to have educed \"the immortal soul of Italy .  clothed in quenchless light\".  On 1 November 1926 Tagore arrived in Hungary and spent some time on the shore of Lake Balaton in the city of Balatonfüred, recovering from heart problems at a sanitarium"
