      "subSubject": "Books & Authors",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "In 1927, Tagore travelled for four months to _____, stopping in British Malaya, Siam and the Dutch East Indies",
      "answer": "Southeast Asia",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " clothed in quenchless light\".  On 1 November 1926 Tagore arrived in Hungary and spent some time on the shore of Lake Balaton in the city of Balatonfüred, recovering from heart problems at a sanitarium.  He planted a tree, and a bust statue was placed there in 1956 (a gift from the Indian government, the work of Rasithan Kashar, replaced by a newly gifted statue in 2005) and the lakeside promenade still bears his name since 1957.  In 1927, Tagore travelled for four months to Southeast Asia, stopping in British Malaya, Siam and the Dutch East Indies.  Tagore was accompanied by the linguist Suniti Kumar Chatterji and visited the Buddhist shrine of Borobudur.  The goal of the tour was to raise funds for Visva-Bharati University and explore the legacies of ancient Indian art, culture and religion in the region.  The journey had a profound influence on Tagore’s view of Indian history and he became an enthusiastic supporter and promoter of the work of the Calcutta-based Greater India Society, acting as its Purodha or honourary chief advisor"
    },
    {
      "id": "q4715",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Books & Authors",
      "region": "",
