      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The play Dak Ghar (The Post Office; 1912), describes the child Amal defying his stuffy and puerile confines by ultimately \"fall[ing] asleep\", hinting his physical death.  A story with borderless appeal—gleaning rave reviews in Europe—Dak Ghar dealt with death as, in Tagore's words, \"spiritual freedom\" from \"the world of hoarded wealth and certified creeds\".  Another is Tagore's Chandalika (Untouchable Girl), which was modelled on an ancient Buddhist legend describing how Ananda, the Gautama Buddha's disciple, asks a tribal girl for water.  In Raktakarabi (\"Red\" or \"Blood Oleanders\") is an allegorical struggle against a kleptocrat king who rules over the residents of Yaksha puri.  Chitrangada, Chandalika, and Shyama are other key plays that have dance-drama adaptations, which together are known as Rabindra Nritya Natya.  === Short stories === Tagore began his career in short stories in 1877—when he was only sixteen—with \"Bhikharini\" (\"The Beggar Woman\").  With this, Tagore effectively invented the Bengali-language short story genre"
    },
