      "question": "Chidambaram, who was then part of the _____",
      "answer": "Tamil Maanila Congress",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " === Post-liberalization === Manmohan Singh, under Prime Minister P.  Narasimha Rao, opened the Indian economy in his annual budgets from 1992 to 1993, encouraging foreign investments and reducing the peak import duty from over 300% to 50%.  After the 1996 elections, a non-Congress ministry assumed office, and the financial budget for 1996–97 was presented by P.  Chidambaram, who was then part of the Tamil Maanila Congress.  Following a constitutional crisis when the I.  Gujral Ministry was on its way out, a special session of Parliament was convened solely to pass Chidambaram's 1997–98 budget, which was passed without debate.  After the general elections in March 1998, which led to the Bharatiya Janata Party forming the Central Government, Yashwant Sinha, the then Finance Minister, presented both the interim and final budgets for 1998–99"
    },
    {
      "id": "q4937",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Announcements",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:06:14.935Z",
      "subject": "Announcements",
      "subSubject": "Announcements",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____ was on its way out, a special session of Parliament was convened solely to pass Chidambaram's 1997–98 budget, which was passed without debate",
      "answer": "Gujral Ministry",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " After the 1996 elections, a non-Congress ministry assumed office, and the financial budget for 1996–97 was presented by P.  Chidambaram, who was then part of the Tamil Maanila Congress.  Following a constitutional crisis when the I.  Gujral Ministry was on its way out, a special session of Parliament was convened solely to pass Chidambaram's 1997–98 budget, which was passed without debate.  After the general elections in March 1998, which led to the Bharatiya Janata Party forming the Central Government, Yashwant Sinha, the then Finance Minister, presented both the interim and final budgets for 1998–99.  Following the general elections in 1999, Sinha again became the Finance Minister and presented four annual budgets from 1999–2000 to 2002–2003.  Due to the elections in May 2004, an interim budget was presented by Jaswant Singh"
    },
    {
      "id": "q4938",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Announcements",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:06:14.936Z",
      "subject": "Announcements",
      "subSubject": "Announcements",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Following the general elections in 1999, Sinha again became the _____ and presented four annual budgets from 1999–2000 to 2002–2003",
      "answer": "Finance Minister",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Following a constitutional crisis when the I.  Gujral Ministry was on its way out, a special session of Parliament was convened solely to pass Chidambaram's 1997–98 budget, which was passed without debate.  After the general elections in March 1998, which led to the Bharatiya Janata Party forming the Central Government, Yashwant Sinha, the then Finance Minister, presented both the interim and final budgets for 1998–99.  Following the general elections in 1999, Sinha again became the Finance Minister and presented four annual budgets from 1999–2000 to 2002–2003.  Due to the elections in May 2004, an interim budget was presented by Jaswant Singh.  The Union Budget of India for 2012–2013 was presented by Pranab Mukherjee on 16 March 2012, marking the seventh budget of his career.  These budgetary proposals were applicable for the financial year from 1 April 2012 to 31 March 2013"
    },
