      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:06:26.957Z",
      "subject": "RBI Press Releases",
      "subSubject": "RBI Press Releases",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "During the First World War (_____–1918) through the end of the Second World War (1939–1945), and two years thereafter until the independence of India were challenging for Indian ba",
      "answer": "1914",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The inaugural officeholder was the Britisher Sir Osborne Smith(1 April 1935), while C.  Deshmukh(11 August 1943) was the first Indian governor.  On 12 December 2018, Shaktikanta Das, who was the finance secretary with the Government of India, begins his journey as the new RBI Governor, taking charge from Urjit R Patel.  During the First World War (1914–1918) through the end of the Second World War (1939–1945), and two years thereafter until the independence of India were challenging for Indian banking.  The years of the First World War were turbulent, and it took its toll with banks simply collapsing despite the Indian economy gaining indirect boost due to war-related economic activities.  At least 94 banks in India failed between 1913 and 1918 as indicated in the following table: == Post-Independence == During 1938–46, bank branch offices trebled to 3,469 and deposits quadrupled to ₹ 962 crore.  Nevertheless, the partition of India in 1947 adversely impacted the economies of Punjab and West Bengal, paralysing banking activities for months"
    },
