      "fact": " During the First World War (1914–1918) through the end of the Second World War (1939–1945), and two years thereafter until the independence of India were challenging for Indian banking.  The years of the First World War were turbulent, and it took its toll with banks simply collapsing despite the Indian economy gaining indirect boost due to war-related economic activities.  At least 94 banks in India failed between 1913 and 1918 as indicated in the following table: == Post-Independence == During 1938–46, bank branch offices trebled to 3,469 and deposits quadrupled to ₹ 962 crore.  Nevertheless, the partition of India in 1947 adversely impacted the economies of Punjab and West Bengal, paralysing banking activities for months.  India's independence marked the end of a regime of the Laissez-faire for the Indian banking.  The Government of India initiated measures to play an active role in the economic life of the nation, and the Industrial Policy Resolution adopted by the government in 1948 envisaged a mixed economy.  This resulted in greater involvement of the state in different segments of the economy including banking and finance"
